00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.260
Let's pop the hood on rig rope.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:33.600
Everyone treats multi-dimensional rope as a simple extension that just splits channels into temporal, height and width subspaces.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:43.420
In practice, the static assignment creates cross-modal interference whenever a text token attends to an image token whose spatial offset is undefined.

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:56.820
RIG rope solves this by adding a modality indicator and visual instance identifier to every token, then gating height width rotations so they only apply inside the same visual instance.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:09.980
Temporal coordinates switch from equal step counters to interpolated cumulative block durations using a logarithmic spatial scale for images and an extra logarithmic extension for video frames.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:17.740
The change adds only constant metadata per token inside tiled attention kernels and requires no learned parameters.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:31.960
The paper supplies a gauge invariance argument showing why cross instance spatial rotation must be avoided and an impossibility result proving static IDs cannot work under shared HW subspaces.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:38.700
It also gives a duration consistency argument against treating every token type as an equal step counter.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:48.860
The key engineering trade-off is that models below roughly 7 billion parameters see little benefit because they lack the capacity to exploit the cleaner geometry.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:07:02.840
While larger models gain more stable long-context multimodal behavior, use RIG ropey when building interleaved vision language agents that must maintain spatial and temporal consistency across many turns.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:10.600
Stick with standard MROP for single modality text workloads where the extra metadata overhead is unnecessary.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:18.740
The formulation supports direct implementation inside existing attention kernels without retraining the underlying model weights.

